    "https://viu.ca/programs/academic-career-preparation/aboriginal-university-bridging-program-certificate": {
      "title": "Indigenous University Bridging",
      "description": "Indigenous University Bridging | Vancouver Island University | Canada Skip to main content Program search icon Search Menu About Programs Admissions & Aid Student Life Support VIU Research About About Get to know VIU. As a teaching university and a research university, we offer a unique experience to students. About VIU Why VIU About VIU President's Office Indigenous Education and Engagement Department Directory Governance, Administration and Planning Accreditation Careers Contact Us Why VIU? Equity, Diversity, Inclusion and Accessibility Sustainability News Events Key Facts Explore VIU's impact From classrooms to research and community engagement, the VIU community's impact is worth celebrating. VIU Impact Report Programs Programs Explore a wide range of program offerings and learning services. Choose from degrees, vocational training, certificates and more. Find your program Learning Services Programs Learning at VIU Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Trades and Applied Technology Programs Academic and Career Preparation programs Professional and Life Long Learning Learning Services Advising Centre Upgrading support Library Talk to a recruiter Explore your options with one of our friendly recruiters. Online and in-person meetings available. Connect with a recruiter Admissions & Aid Admissions & Aid Discover how to apply to VIU and get more information about tuition and financial aid. Admissions Tuition and Aid Admissions VIU Admissions Transfer Admissions Mature Students and Adult Learner Admissions Events and Information Sessions Tuition and Aid VIU Tuition and Aid Scholarships, Awards & Bursaries Student Loans and Grants External Awards and Funding Tuition and Fees How to become a student Make your VIU application successful by following these steps to becoming a student. Steps to become a student Student Life Student Life Make the best of your time at VIU. Get involved in campus events and enjoy island adventures. Student Life Student Services Student Life VIU Mariners Athletics Get to Know Vancouver Island Recreation Co-Curricular Engagement Student Housing Events Calendar Student Services and Support Experiential Learning Accessibility Services Learning Matters Campus Store Food Services Health and Wellness Get ready for the new student orientation Attend in-person and online events, explore campus and connect with your professors and soon-to-be classmates! RockVIU Support VIU Support VIU Connect with other VIU alumni and learn about the impact of donor generosity at VIU. Give VIU Alumni Alumni VIU Alumni Benefits VIU Donors Giving to VIU VIU Foundation Student Success Stories Your support means so much to VIU students Our donors fund over 2000 scholarships, awards, and bursaries each year. Research Research Discover how VIU's interdisciplinary research is making a real-world impact. Research Research Centres and Institutes News and Events VIU Expert List Undergraduate Research Explore VIU's impact From classrooms to research and community engagement, the VIU community's impact is worth celebrating. VIU Impact Report Search VIU Search Search icon Apply to VIU Request Info Information for Future Students Current Students International Students Parents and Families Employees and Faculty Alumni Donors Quick links My Student Record Course Timetables VIULearn Library Parking Health and Wellness Centre Technology @ VIU Employee Portal Campuses Nanaimo (Main Campus) Cowichan tiwšɛmawtxʷ (Powell River) Indigenous University Bridging Breadcrumb Programs and Courses Areas of Study Academic and Career Preparation and High School Equivalency (ABE) Menu CLOSE X OG menu Programs and Courses Areas of Study Open submenu Courses Start Your Application Close submenu Areas of Study Academic and Career Preparation and High School Equivalency (ABE) Art, Design and Performing Arts Arts, Humanities and Social Sciences Business and Management Co-operative Education Education English Language Learning (ELL) Exploratory University Studies (EXPO) Graduate and Post-degree Programs Health Sciences Human Services Indigenous Online Programs and Courses Professional Development and Training Science, Engineering, and Technology Programs Tourism, Recreation and Hospitality Trades and Applied Technology Access site pages through the menu. Got it! Apply Program calendar icon Program Length 1 Year Program credential icon Credential Certificate Program location icon Location Offered Cowichan Nanaimo Aboriginal students seeking to bridge the gap between post-secondary education and their educational aspirations can begin their journey at VIU. The Aboriginal University Bridging Certificate is a customizable program for Indigenous students who want to upgrade their courses and acquire the necessary prerequisites to begin their desired post-secondary education programs. Select the Adult Basic Education courses you need to meet pre-requisite requirements, and consider the option of taking additional entry-level university courses. You will find the support and encouragement of instructors who care about your success, Aboriginal Elders in Residence who can support and guide you on your path, and a network of staff and other students that provide a true sense of belonging. The Indigenous University Bridging Certificate Program The Indigenous University Bridging Program (IUBP) is a customizable program that is designed to support Indigenous adult learners who need to upgrade their education before entering university-level courses and programs. The program provides a supportive, culturally grounded learning environment where students build confidence, strengthen academic skills, and prepare for the transition into a post-secondary program. Learners upgrade skills while receiving guidance and encouragement throughout their educational journey. The program is guided by the First Peoples Principles of Learning , recognizing that learning is holistic, relational, experiential, and rooted in lived experience. Students are supported as whole people and encouraged to bring their identities, knowledge, and experiences into the learning process. Learning happens through relationship, reflection, and connection to community and place. By valuing Indigenous ways of knowing alongside academic learning, the Indigenous University Bridging Program helps students prepare not only for university studies, but for long-term success in education and beyond. What is the IUBP about? Holistic personal and academic growth that nurtures spiritual, emotional, mental, and physical well-being Culturally responsive access to post-secondary prerequisite courses Learning in a safe, welcoming, and supportive community Learning in circle and in relationship with others who share common goals, values, and experiences Goal setting and educational planning grounded in personal vision, purpose, and community connection Academic skill development supported through Indigenous perspectives Collaborative and relational learning, including group work rooted in reciprocity and shared responsibility Building confidence, self-determination, and leadership capacity in preparation for future academic pathways Students are supported by culturally grounded services, including an ACP Student Success Advisor – Indigenous Specialty, and the dedicated team at Shq’apthut, who provide wraparound supports rooted in Indigenous knowledge systems, cultural safety, and community care. Program Curriculum To obtain your IUBP certificate, you will need to complete: ILRP 100 - (Indigenous Learning and Recognition Portfolio) A documented and agreed upon individually selected program that includes: At least 4 Adult Basic Education courses at the 04/06 level (grade 11/12), or, At least 3 Adult Basic Education courses at the 04/06 (grade 11/12) level and STCE 110 - (Striving and Thriving in Colonized Education) Adult Basic Education (ABE) Courses Courses are available starting from literacy levels up to Grade 12 including Biology, Chemistry, English, Mathematics, Physics, Education and Career Planning, and Study Skills. Please see: ABE course descriptions and prerequisites. Note: Availability of course options are subject to funding and enrollment. For more information, please contact our ACP Student Success Advisors . Required University-Level Courses ILRP 100 - (Indigenous Learning and Recognition Portfolio) Optional University-Level Courses STCE 110 - (Striving and Thriving in Colonized Education) is suggested as part of program completion. Students may have the opportunity to take other introductory university-level courses or participate in orientations to VIU programs, helping them build familiarity with post-secondary expectations and learning environments. Financial Support You may qualify for the Adult Upgrading Grant (AUG) and/or other financial supports. Talk to our ACP Student Success Advisors for more information or contact Financial Aid and Awards . If you are planning on seeking sponsorship from your nation, the government, or another agency, please ensure they complete VIU’s Application for Sponsorship . Program Outline To obtain your IUBP certificate, you will need to complete: ILRP 100 - (Indigenous Learning and Recognition Portfolio) A documented and agreed upon individually selected program that includes: At least 4 Adult Basic Education courses at the 04/06 level (grade 11/12), or, At least 3 Adult Basic Education courses at the 04/06 (grade 11/12) level and STCE 110 - (Striving and Thriving in Colonized Education) Talk to our ACP Student Success Advisors to review what courses you need to obtain your IUBP certificate. VIU offers a wide variety of ABE courses. Not all courses will be available every term. Our ACP Student Success Advisors will help you choose the courses you need. Ask them about online and evening courses if you need more flexibility in your schedule. Admission Requirements This program is designed to support students who identify as Indigenous in Canada, including First Nations, Metis, and Inuit peoples. All ABE courses have prerequisites. Students must meet the prerequisites for each course. You can meet these prerequisites in two ways: through providing a recent high school/post-secondary transcript (within 5 years) or by taking an ABE assessment. Students must be 18 years or older or have special permission. Note: An ABE assessment may be required for placement purposes prior to course registration. For questions, please contact our ACP Student Success Advisors . Program Fees Domestic Fees Tuition and Other Mandatory Student Fees When applying to the program, applicants will be charged a non-refundable application fee . When applying to graduate, students will be charged a non-refundable graduation and alumni fee . Some courses have additional fees to pay for extraordinary class–related expenses. Fees - Two Semesters Amount Tuition (ABE courses x $0 + 10 academic credits x $173.12 per credit) 1,731.20 Student Activity fee (8 months x $20.78 per month) 166.24 Student Services fee (8 months x $22.94 per month) 183.52 VIU Students' Union fee (8 months x $50.08 per month) 400.64 Health and Dental Plan fee ($285.00 per year) 285.00 Total 2,766.60 Notes The above table is based on two 3-credit courses and four ABE courses over two semesters. Students taking additional courses should adjust the fee total accordingly. Approximate cost for books and supplies: $50 - $150 per semester. Costs vary depending on courses selected. The VIUSU Health and Dental Plan fee is assessed for all students enrolled in 6 credits or more per term, or in Trades/Vocational programs of 5 months or longer. All fees are subject to change without prior notice and are expected to increase by 2% each year on April 1. Start Date, Deadlines, and Additional Application Details Domestic (Canadian) Program Start Date Accepting Applications Campus Sep 8, 2026 Ongoing Nanaimo Application Fee: $46.98 Waitlist: No Testing Required: No Admissions Officer: Name: Donna Kist Phone: 250.740.6400 Email: Donna.Kist@viu.ca Area Contact: Name: Sara Fulla Email: ABE.Advising@viu.ca Comments: Applications are now being accepted through Education Planner BC. Paper applications can be downloaded or printed. Apply to VIU . Please contact Sara Fulla, Student Success Advisor - Indigenous Specialty, for further information. You can reach Sara by email to ABE.Advising@viu.ca Sep 8, 2026 Ongoing Cowichan Application Fee: 46.98 Admissions Officer: Name: Zoë Doiron Phone: 250.746.3512 Email: Zoe.Doiron@viu.ca Area Contact: Name: Shiyu Huang Phone: 250.746.3500 Email: ABE.Cowichan@viu.ca Comments: Applications are now being accepted through Education Planner BC. Paper applications can be downloaded or printed. Apply to VIU . Please contact Shiyu Huang, Student Success Advisor, for further information. You can reach Shiyu by email at ABE.Cowichan@viu.ca Offered part-time at the Cowichan Campus. Sign up to learn more about VIU Previous versions of this program can be found in the Program Archive . phone-square phone-square icon Connect with Us! Phone or email us info-circle info-circle icon Events and Info Sessions View events pen-square pen-square icon Ready to join us at VIU? Start Your application facebook-square Facebook instagram Instagram youtube Youtube linkedin Linked-in bluesky Bluesky Vancouver Island University Nanaimo (Main Campus) 900 Fifth Street Nanaimo, BC V9R 5S5 Canada 250.753.3245 1.888.920.2221 Campus Map Parking Campuses Campuses Nanaimo (main campus) Cowichan tiwšɛmawtxʷ (Powell River) Footer Buttons Primary News Give to VIU Footer Buttons Secondary Events Media Careers The VIU community acknowledges and thanks the Snuneymuxw, Quw’utsun, Tla’amin, Snaw-naw-as and Qualicum First Nation on whose traditional lands we teach, learn, research, live and share knowledge. VIU terms menu Accreditation Terms Emergency Information Website Feedback copyright 2026 Vancouver Island University"
    },
